WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:14.000
 Thank you, Lane. Asalaamu Alaikum. Shalom. Waap'pain. Pazu D'Iwario. Ventre.

00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:21.000
 And what's the Indian? Shunai. Shunai from India.

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:25.020
 And any other language that I forgot, I'll meet it for you later on this

00:00:25.020 --> 00:00:29.460
 evening. I want to first as always thank the creator of the universe, who we

00:00:29.460 --> 00:00:32.000
 know by many names I've referred to God as Allah.

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:36.850
 And we know that the name of God is a linguistic terminology that always means

00:00:36.850 --> 00:00:41.110
 the most high. The creator who has brought us all up a state of non-existence

00:00:41.110 --> 00:00:46.020
 and given us life, and given us spirits, and given us souls, and given us minds,

00:00:46.020 --> 00:00:50.000
 and given us more inside of ourselves than we see outside of ourselves.

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:54.080
 And we're eternally grateful to the creator for not just giving us lives, but

00:00:54.080 --> 00:00:58.050
 also giving us guidance through all the various scriptures and all the heroes

00:00:58.050 --> 00:01:02.490
 and sheroes that brought inspiration to humanity all over the world about the

00:01:02.490 --> 00:01:06.000
 importance of spirituality, about the importance of self and power.

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:10.650
 And I would be remissive and acknowledge and bear witness to those whose

00:01:10.650 --> 00:01:15.580
 shoulders that I stand on, who paid a mighty price for us to be here today. I

00:01:15.580 --> 00:01:20.970
 am a descendant of First Nation people on my father's side who were Cherokee,

00:01:20.970 --> 00:01:26.650
 who were brought here on the left side of the mountains, as they say, from the

00:01:26.650 --> 00:01:30.000
 rocky side over to the flat side of Tennessee and Alaska.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:34.620
 And my great grandfather was one of the great slaves who was descendants of the

00:01:34.620 --> 00:01:39.110
 Gambian cynical reasons of Africa. And I thank Almighty God for being a

00:01:39.110 --> 00:01:43.470
 descendant of them and being able to understand and acknowledge my ancestors in

00:01:43.470 --> 00:01:47.000
 the way that so many African Americans have not been able to.

00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:53.850
 And so I bear witness to this struggle. They didn't come here by way of the Mayflower.

00:01:53.850 --> 00:01:59.690
 They didn't come here by way of Ellis Island. They came here in the halls of

00:01:59.690 --> 00:02:01.440
 slave ship. And we should always recognize and understand the importance of

00:02:01.440 --> 00:02:03.420
 that travel from the west coast of Africa to the east coast of America and that

00:02:03.420 --> 00:02:05.000
 sojourn here in North America.

00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:09.250
 So we understand the brilliance of the diversity of this country, but also the

00:02:09.250 --> 00:02:12.950
 brilliance of what we call the resilience of all of our people who have

00:02:12.950 --> 00:02:16.690
 practiced history and thank Almighty God for them. And thank you for being here

00:02:16.690 --> 00:02:17.000
 tonight.

00:02:17.000 --> 00:02:22.430
 I want to give a big shout out to the Hanson family, who has really been, I can't

00:02:22.430 --> 00:02:27.270
 even imagine where we would be without the ambassadors of this international

00:02:27.270 --> 00:02:29.000
 gathering at the Soul Corps.

00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:35.590
 And may we continue to acknowledge their work and may they continue to do work

00:02:35.590 --> 00:02:41.900
 that they're doing to great gatherings like this that where we celebrate

00:02:41.900 --> 00:02:47.810
 diversity and celebrate ourselves as ethnic Americans in this family park

00:02:47.810 --> 00:02:49.000
 called America.

00:02:49.000 --> 00:02:53.020
 And I also want to say I will be reduced to the Valley of the College and

00:02:53.020 --> 00:02:57.540
 recognize the city itself and these great guys that are here this evening that

00:02:57.540 --> 00:03:01.000
 also represent the importance of unity and diversity.

00:03:01.000 --> 00:03:06.030
 Not just not uniformity, but uniformity in the fact that they got the sinners

00:03:06.030 --> 00:03:10.540
 of those who came here and struggled and made a life for themselves and

00:03:10.540 --> 00:03:13.000
 celebrate themselves as well.

00:03:13.000 --> 00:03:17.740
 It's very important that we celebrate diversity and understand the importance

00:03:17.740 --> 00:03:21.330
 of this struggle. I also want to say that it's important for these kinds of

00:03:21.330 --> 00:03:24.000
 events to take place because Cleveland is a pivotal city.

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:28.190
 Obviously in the next couple of months the whole world will be looking at us

00:03:28.190 --> 00:03:32.450
 and all of us are ambassadors and we continue to be ambassadors even not into

00:03:32.450 --> 00:03:37.050
 the Republican National Convention, but also afterwards because a lot of eyes

00:03:37.050 --> 00:03:38.000
 will be on Cleveland.

00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:42.600
 And I know that we want to make sure that we shine not just for the city, but

00:03:42.600 --> 00:03:47.000
 for what we represent as those involved in this diversity issue.

00:03:47.000 --> 00:03:52.520
 My grandmother used to say there are people who make things happen, there are

00:03:52.520 --> 00:03:57.000
 people who watch things happen, and there are some people who say what happened.

00:03:57.000 --> 00:04:00.000
 I don't want you to be that group.

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:04.790
 She always encouraged me to make sure that I made something happen in life for

00:04:04.790 --> 00:04:09.180
 myself and make sure that I try to make a difference in the lives of other

00:04:09.180 --> 00:04:10.000
 people.

00:04:10.000 --> 00:04:15.190
 My grandmother came here as I said before. Her mother was a full-blooded

00:04:15.190 --> 00:04:18.000
 Cherokee and she was from Pulaski.

00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:22.170
 And if you're not familiar with Pulaski, Pulaski was the birthplace of the Ku

00:04:22.170 --> 00:04:26.000
 Klux Klan. So here we've got our Pulaski and came to Cleveland.

00:04:26.000 --> 00:04:30.840
 But she became an entrepreneur and she sold magazines and books and newspapers

00:04:30.840 --> 00:04:37.110
 from various cities throughout the United States that were from the black press,

00:04:37.110 --> 00:04:41.000
 from Baltimore, from Detroit, from St. Louis and all these different places.

00:04:41.000 --> 00:04:45.430
 But she also sold the plane dealer and I would have to come here early in the

00:04:45.430 --> 00:04:50.210
 morning before sunrise to help put these papers together so she could sell that

00:04:50.210 --> 00:04:51.000
 plane dealer.

00:04:51.000 --> 00:04:54.480
 And I would sit there and read all these other newspapers from other cities

00:04:54.480 --> 00:04:56.000
 throughout the United States.

00:04:56.000 --> 00:05:00.640
 And I saw that there was so much commonality in various things that were going

00:05:00.640 --> 00:05:04.000
 on throughout urban America and the United States.

00:05:04.000 --> 00:05:08.020
 And that is what inspired me to find out more about my roots. I began to read

00:05:08.020 --> 00:05:11.000
 books about classical African civilization.

00:05:11.000 --> 00:05:15.630
 I began to read books about the Moors that had lived in Spain and helped civilize

00:05:15.630 --> 00:05:20.000
 possibly even when there was no understanding of who the Moors were.

00:05:20.000 --> 00:05:25.360
 But we know now that we hear about cities like Toledo, we hear about places

00:05:25.360 --> 00:05:29.000
 like Cordova, which is not just a car, it's a city.

00:05:29.000 --> 00:05:33.020
 There's a city down the street called Medina. A lot of people don't remember it.

00:05:33.020 --> 00:05:35.000
 It used to be called Medina at one time.

00:05:35.000 --> 00:05:40.330
 So we began to associate things with language by picking up on all of these

00:05:40.330 --> 00:05:44.810
 markers of linguistic similarity and then we can understand why it's important

00:05:44.810 --> 00:05:46.000
 to celebrate ourselves.

00:05:46.000 --> 00:05:50.960
 So I chose the name Callebso Mod for myself after studying what the name is and

00:05:50.960 --> 00:05:53.000
 then began to try to make things happen.

00:05:53.000 --> 00:05:56.830
 And for the last 40 years, as a result of my family and friends and the support

00:05:56.830 --> 00:06:00.000
 group that I have here, we've been able to do many things.

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:04.380
 But we want to do more. And one of the most important things that we want to do

00:06:04.380 --> 00:06:09.980
 is to show young men and women in general, but African Americans in particular,

00:06:09.980 --> 00:06:12.000
 that they can transform themselves.

00:06:12.000 --> 00:06:17.490
 They can visualize a future for themselves. If they can visualize it and

00:06:17.490 --> 00:06:21.000
 believe it, then their willpower will allow them to achieve it.

00:06:21.000 --> 00:06:24.850
 And it's important that we continue to ring that bell in their minds about

00:06:24.850 --> 00:06:28.000
 transformation because everybody's got a tuning fork.

00:06:28.000 --> 00:06:31.350
 Everybody's got a tuning fork. And many of the people in the population that we

00:06:31.350 --> 00:06:35.000
 work with, they've covered their work up with a lot of other things.

00:06:35.000 --> 00:06:40.600
 There's a lot of trauma in our communities. There's a lot of mental health,

00:06:40.600 --> 00:06:45.000
 social health. There's a lot of economic, unhealthy economic communities.

00:06:45.000 --> 00:06:49.580
 And so what we need to do, what we have to do, is to go and cut through all of

00:06:49.580 --> 00:06:54.140
 that and show them that the tuning fork that can be here can ignite them to go

00:06:54.140 --> 00:06:59.000
 through a transformation process like the caterpillar to the butterfly.

00:06:59.000 --> 00:07:04.180
 That caterpillar goes into cocoon and uses its own body in order to make that

00:07:04.180 --> 00:07:07.000
 cocoon. And then out of that comes a butterfly.

00:07:07.000 --> 00:07:12.000
 That to me is a very beautiful picture of transformation of what can happen.

00:07:12.000 --> 00:07:14.000
 And let's continue to work on that.

00:07:14.000 --> 00:07:17.390
 I will continue to work with people like Dan and everybody. We see so many

00:07:17.390 --> 00:07:18.000
 familiar faces.

00:07:18.000 --> 00:07:22.020
 I remember Dan Coughlin when I was a young man playing basketball. He said,

00:07:22.020 --> 00:07:27.090
 back in the day, we were playing dealer and a lot of the Dick Russ and a lot of

00:07:27.090 --> 00:07:28.000
 the long ground.

00:07:28.000 --> 00:07:31.060
 And we worked not just in the hood, so to speak, even though we do a lot of

00:07:31.060 --> 00:07:32.000
 work in the hood.

00:07:32.000 --> 00:07:35.390
 It's important we understand we're global people and that we're just as

00:07:35.390 --> 00:07:39.990
 comfortable in a sloppy village as we are in Little Italy, as we are in Polastan,

00:07:39.990 --> 00:07:44.000
 as we are in the African American communities, as we are in the Asian Plaza.

00:07:44.000 --> 00:07:47.500
 And as we continue to focus on what we have in common with each other, then I

00:07:47.500 --> 00:07:51.000
 think we can be successful not only in this mic, but also in the next.

00:07:51.000 --> 00:07:55.520
 Thank you very much. I love you. And let's thank you for our rest of the tuning

00:07:55.520 --> 00:07:56.000
 work.

00:07:58.000 --> 00:08:01.760
 [APPLAUSE]

